  "body": "Filing this here so we can track it on the whatwg side.\n\nIn https://github.com/w3c/html/issues/250 @travisleithead wrote\n\n> Per the thread here: https://www.w3.org/Bugs/Public/show_bug.cgi?id=25140 (now resolved) I think we have an action item recorded in this isssue.\n> \n> @stevefaulkner @zcorpan\n> Looks like the following proposal had traction at the time:\n> \n> ``` html\n> <details>\n>   <input id=\"anonymous-control\">\n>   <summary id=\"summary-anonymous-control\">\n>     <label for=\"summary-anonymous-control\">label text</label>\n>   </summary>\n>   Some content \n> </details>\n> ```\n> \n> It allows the label to provide a larger hit-target on platforms where this is not the default convention, and also provides a label for the anonymous control used by the UA to perform the toggle. We need to describe the prose for how this works, and provide at least one example.\n\n(`<input id=\"anonymous-control\">` is the UA-generated disclosure widget, but the `label` is by the author.)\n\nMy position is summed up with\n\n> I think it's a bad idea to work around accessibility failures in OS X (and others) when designing new HTML features. It would be better for OS X (and others) to fix their accessibility failures so that all disclosure triangles are usable by people with motor disabilities, not just Web apps that use &lt;dialog>.\n\nfrom https://www.w3.org/Bugs/Public/show_bug.cgi?id=25140#c6\n\nbut I also think it would be OK to allow `<label for=summary-element>` to give a label for the disclosure widget, if that is something that implementors want instead of just making disclosure widgets bigger (for hit testing; maybe as an accessibility setting?).\n",
